__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,_e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(_e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),_e.PrivacyPolicyBody=function(){const t=(0,e.c)(4);let o,c,y,p;t[0]===Symbol.for("react.memo_cache_sentinel")?(o=(0,l.jsx)(n.Text,{variant:"title",style:{marginTop:n.spacing.sm},children:"Privacy Policy"}),t[0]=o):o=t[0];t[1]===Symbol.for("react.memo_cache_sentinel")?(c=(0,l.jsx)(n.Text,{variant:"caption",color:"textMuted",style:{marginTop:n.spacing.xs,marginBottom:n.spacing.lg},children:"Last updated: [publication date \u2014 to be added]"}),t[1]=c):c=t[1];t[2]===Symbol.for("react.memo_cache_sentinel")?(y={marginBottom:n.spacing.lg},t[2]=y):y=t[2];t[3]===Symbol.for("react.memo_cache_sentinel")?(p=(0,l.jsxs)(s.default,{testID:"fm-privacy-policy-body",children:[o,c,(0,l.jsxs)(n.Card,{glow:!0,style:y,children:[(0,l.jsx)(n.Eyebrow,{icon:"sparkles",label:"In short",style:{marginBottom:n.spacing.sm}}),(0,l.jsx)(n.Text,{variant:"body",color:"textMuted",children:"This describes what Cluster collects, why, who within your family can see it, and what happens to it if you delete your account. It is written to match exactly what the app actually stores."})]}),h.map(u)]}),t[3]=p):p=t[3];return p};var e=r(d[0]);r(d[1]);var t,o=r(d[2]),s=(t=o)&&t.__esModule?t:{default:t},n=r(d[3]),l=r(d[4]);const h=[{heading:'Who we are',paragraphs:['Cluster is operated by [the app owner\u2019s legal entity name and address \u2014 to be added]. Questions about this policy or your data: [contact email \u2014 to be added].']},{heading:'What we collect, and why',paragraphs:['Cluster is a shared household app: everything you and your family put into it is stored so that the rest of your family can see it too. That sharing is the product, not a side effect.','Account & sign-in: your email address, and either a securely hashed password or the fact that you signed in with Google, Microsoft, Apple or Facebook. We never see or store your password for any of those accounts.','Your profile: a display name, an optional avatar image, and the timezone and language your device reported. Your display name and avatar are visible to anyone who shares a family with you. Your timezone is used to make reminders and quiet hours land at the correct local time, and to tell whether travel time and address search are available where you are (see \u201cWhere travel time isn\u2019t available\u201d, below).','Family content: lists, list items, calendar events and reminders, with whatever text you typed into them. This is visible to every member of the family it belongs to, unless it\u2019s on a calendar you marked private, in which case it\u2019s visible only to you.','Places and meeting links: an event, a reminder or an item in your stash can have a place, either as the text you typed or, if you picked it from address search, its name and address with Google\u2019s identifier for that place (a \u201cplace ID\u201d). An event can also have a meeting link, such as a Zoom, Google Meet or Microsoft Teams link. A place or meeting link is part of its event or reminder, so it\u2019s visible to exactly the same people; a stash item\u2019s place is visible only to you, like the stash item. A meeting link can also arrive with an event from Google Calendar or a calendar feed. A meeting link you add in Cluster is never sent to Google.','Home and travel settings: if you use travel time, you can save a Home address and choose how you usually travel, how much earlier you like to leave, whether to be told when it\u2019s time to go, which roads to avoid on car routes, and whether a trip may start from where you are. On any event or reminder you can also choose a different way of getting there or a different amount of time to spare. These settings and choices are yours alone. Nobody else in any of your groups can see them, including an owner.','Your Home\u2019s position: with Cluster Plus, a trip from Home starts from your Home\u2019s position on the map, not from its address, and your Home\u2019s weather is looked up from it too. To find that position, our servers look your Home up with Google Maps Platform once, round the answer to about 110 metres (three decimal places of latitude and longitude), and keep that rounded position for up to 30 days, after which it is looked up again. It is cleared as soon as you change or remove your Home, and, like your Home, nobody else can see it.','Travel times: with Cluster Plus, Cluster works out how long it will take you to get to each event or reminder with a place that you\u2019re going to, and when you need to leave. Each travel time is worked out for you alone, from where you would be coming from (your Home, the place of your previous event, or where you are), and only you can see it. We store each result so the app can draw it and remind you: how long the trip takes, how far it is, when to leave, and what it starts from by name (\u201cHome\u201d, the place of your previous event, or \u201cyour location\u201d). We delete it a week after the trip.','Turning travel time off: travel time is on unless you turn it off, which you can do at any time in Settings \u2192 Travel \u2192 Travel time. While it\u2019s off, Cluster works out no travel times for you and sends none of your places, your Home or your position to Google Maps Platform for them. The weather has its own switch (Weather, below), so it carries on unless you turn it off too. Your upcoming travel times are deleted, and so are any leave-by alerts not yet sent. Your Home and travel settings are kept, so turning it back on finds them as they were. The switch doesn\u2019t change address search: if you search for an address, what you type is still sent (see \u201cWho else sees it\u201d).','Where travel time isn\u2019t available: travel time, address search and weather aren\u2019t available to people in the European Union, Iceland, Liechtenstein, Norway, the United Kingdom (with the Channel Islands, the Isle of Man and Gibraltar), Switzerland, Monaco, San Marino, the Vatican or Andorra. We tell where you are from the timezone your device reports (your profile, above), never from your location. If your timezone is in one of those places, Cluster sends nothing of yours to Google Maps Platform: no searches, no places, no Home and no position, whether or not you have Cluster Plus. You can still type a place yourself and open it in your maps app with Directions.','Weather: with Cluster Plus, Cluster shows the forecast on your timebar and on events: for your Home, and for an event or reminder that has a place. It isn\u2019t available where travel time isn\u2019t (above). It has its own switch, apart from travel time\u2019s: you can turn it off at any time in Settings \u2192 Travel \u2192 Weather, and while it\u2019s off no forecast is asked for you and nothing of yours is sent to Google Maps Platform for it. For your Home, we use your Home\u2019s rounded position (above), so a forecast never sends your Home\u2019s address or place ID. For an event\u2019s or reminder\u2019s place, we send that place to Google Maps Platform (as a place ID, or as the address text) to find where it is, and round the answer to about 110 metres. We then ask Google\u2019s weather service for the forecast at a point rounded to about 5 km, never the exact address (see \u201cWho else sees it\u201d). We don\u2019t store forecasts: our servers keep one in memory for up to 30 minutes, shared by everyone nearby, and the app keeps it in memory for up to 30 minutes more. It is never saved on your device, so no forecast shows while you\u2019re offline.','On your way: with Cluster Plus, once each morning (at the time you set in Settings \u2192 Travel \u2192 Morning check), Cluster looks through the items in your stash that have a place and tells you which of them are near somewhere you are already going that day. Only you can see what it finds. To do this we send each of those stash items\u2019 places to Google Maps Platform (as a place ID, or as an address in text) to find where it is, round the answer to about 110 metres, and keep that rounded position for up to 30 days, shared with the same kind of position for events and reminders and with nothing that says whose it is. We also ask Google Maps Platform for the route of a trip you are already taking that day, to see how near it passes; we don\u2019t keep the route. What we keep is the result for that day: which stash item, what it is near, how many extra minutes it adds and when it would fit. It is deleted a day or two later. It isn\u2019t available where travel time isn\u2019t (above), and it stops when you turn travel time off, or when you choose Off under Settings \u2192 Travel \u2192 On your way.','Your device\u2019s location: Cluster uses your phone\u2019s or browser\u2019s location only if you allow it, only while Cluster is open, and only for travel: if you switch on Start from where I am, then from an hour before you need to leave for an event or reminder with a place, Cluster sends your position to our servers to work out the trip from where you are. It never sends your position with an address search. Before anything else uses your position, our servers round it to about 110 metres, and it is only that rounded position that goes on to Google Maps Platform, which handles it under Google\u2019s own terms (see \u201cWho else sees it\u201d). We use it to answer that one request and then discard it: we don\u2019t store it, we don\u2019t keep it in our logs, and we never show it to anyone. Cluster never uses your location in the background. You can switch Start from where I am off in Settings \u2192 Travel, or turn off location for Cluster in your device\u2019s settings.','Availability: you can belong to several groups in Cluster (a family, a class, a team), and nothing you put in one is shown in another. The one exception is when you\u2019re busy. When someone who shares a group with you is choosing a time for an event, Cluster shows them the start and end times of events you\u2019re going to or haven\u2019t declined, in any of your groups and on private calendars too. Only the times are shown \u2014 never an event\u2019s title, place, notes, or which group it belongs to. Nothing about your travel is shown either: not your Home, your travel times, or when you leave.','Your voice: if you press and hold the microphone button in the + menu, your phone or browser turns what you say into text using its own speech recognition: Apple\u2019s or Google\u2019s on a phone, your browser\u2019s on the web (Chrome, for one, sends the audio to Google for this). Cluster hears only while you hold the button, never receives or stores the audio, and keeps only the text that you then add to an event or reminder. You can turn the microphone off for Cluster in your device\u2019s settings, and typing works the same without it.','Typing it in plain words: the + menu\u2019s quick entry reads what you type on your device, and nothing leaves the device for that. With Cluster Plus you can also let an AI reader read harder sentences, under \u201cRead entries with AI\u201d in Settings \u2192 Cluster Plus, which you can switch off at any time. It stays off until you have answered a one-time notice that says what it does; if you say Not now, quick entry still works, with the built-in reader. While it\u2019s on, each reading sends the sentence (up to 300 characters), with any place in it, after your device has swapped the names of the members of the cluster the entry is for, the names of that cluster\u2019s lists, and any links, email addresses and phone numbers in it for short placeholders (\u201cfor [m2]\u201d). Only your device knows what each placeholder stands for, and it puts the real words back when the reading comes back. The members and lists themselves go as those placeholders alone, with what kind each list is, so no name, surname or email address of a member is sent. Each reading also sends the local date, time and weekday, and your time zone and language and region setting. When the sentence may be about something already coming up (\u201cmove dentist to 6pm\u201d), it also sends the times of up to ten upcoming events or reminders your sentence may mean, each with only the words of its title that your sentence shares (\u201c\u2026 dentist \u2026\u201d): your device picks the few whose titles share a word with it, so the rest of your calendar is never sent, and each goes under a short label rather than by id. Other words you type go as you typed them: a place, a note, or a name your device doesn\u2019t know as one member\u2019s (someone outside the cluster, or a first name two members share). Our servers check that you have Cluster Plus, count the reading against a limit of 200 a day for you, and send it to Anthropic (see \u201cWho else sees it\u201d). Our servers log only the length of the sentence and how long the reading took, never the sentence itself. Cluster keeps nothing you typed: a reading fills in the card, and nothing is saved until you tap Add. The one-time notice is asked again whenever what is sent grows, as it was when upcoming events and reminders were added. In As you type mode a sentence may be read more than once while you type it, each time you pause. Unlike travel time and the weather, the AI reader has no regional rule: it is available wherever you have Cluster Plus, including in the places listed under \u201cWhere travel time isn\u2019t available\u201d. Nothing is sent for it without Cluster Plus, or while it is off.','Chat: messages you send in your family\u2019s group chat, a smaller group channel, or a direct 1:1 message. A group or family-channel message is visible to that group\u2019s members; a direct message is visible only to the two of you \u2014 never the rest of the family. A thread about one event, list, list item or reminder is visible to everyone in that group who can see the item (for an event on a private calendar, only you), and is deleted with the item. Photos you attach to a chat message are visible to that same audience.','External calendars: if you subscribe a calendar to an external .ics feed, we store the URL you gave us and fetch it on a schedule so events stay current. The feed\u2019s events, with any meeting links in them, are visible to your family the same way any other calendar is.','Google Calendar: if you connect a Google account to sync calendars (you may connect more than one), we ask Google for permission to see your list of calendars, to see and edit events on your calendars, to create a calendar for Cluster, and to know that Google account\u2019s email address. For each account we store its email address and the access token Google issues, and only ever read or change events on the calendars you choose to add. Their events are visible to you alone, or to your family if you share that calendar. An event\u2019s video-call link comes in with it as its meeting link, and changes you make to the events in Cluster are written back to Google. Our use of information received from Google APIs adheres to the Google API Services User Data Policy, including the Limited Use requirements. We use it only to show and sync your calendars and, if you have Cluster Plus, to work out the travel time to the places on those events, which you see on your timebar and in leave-by alerts. For that purpose alone, we send an event\u2019s place to Google Maps Platform to work out the route. We never use it for advertising, and we don\u2019t sell it. Disconnecting an account in Settings (on its cluster\u2019s page, under Google Calendar and feeds) deletes its token and removes its calendars from Cluster; nothing is deleted in Google.','Picture cards: the Calendar\u2019s Visual view shows events as picture cards. The pictures your family chose for events, and any picture rules, are stored and visible to your family (a choice on a private calendar\u2019s event is visible only to you). A photo you add as a picture card, and its name, are visible to every member of your family. Card pictures are chosen on your device from the event\u2019s title, and \u201cRead aloud\u201d uses your device\u2019s own text-to-speech.','Push notifications: a device token identifying your phone or browser, and a queue of pending notifications addressed to you (a reminder due or, with Cluster Plus, the time to leave for an event). The token is sent to Expo\u2019s push notification service, which relays it on to Apple\u2019s or Google\u2019s own delivery infrastructure for your device \u2014 we do not talk to Apple or Google directly for this. Nobody in your family can see your device tokens or notification preferences \u2014 they are yours alone.','Cluster Plus: address search, travel time, the live-traffic refresh, leave-by alerts, the weather and the AI reader of quick entry (see \u201cTyping it in plain words\u201d) are part of Cluster Plus, except where travel features aren\u2019t available (see \u201cWhere travel time isn\u2019t available\u201d, above). It can\u2019t be bought yet: for now, we switch it on by hand or with a code. If you have it, we keep a record of your plan: Individual Plus (for you, in every group you\u2019re in) or Family Plus (for everyone in one group); whose it is (for Family Plus, the person who covers the group, and which group); how it was given (by us, or with a code); when it starts and ends; and whether it was cancelled. If you redeem a code, we also record that you used it, so one person can\u2019t use a code twice. Your Individual Plus is visible only to you. Everyone in a group with Family Plus can see that the group has it, who covers it, and when it ends. When Cluster Plus can be bought, the purchase will go through the App Store, Google Play or a card payment provider, the record will also hold that store\u2019s reference for the purchase, and this policy will be updated before then to say what they share with us.']},{heading:'Who can see what',paragraphs:['Cluster\u2019s entire access model is enforced by the database itself, not by application code that could have a bug in it. Your account credentials, notification settings and device tokens are yours alone, and so are your Home, travel settings, travel choices and travel times. Your profile is visible to anyone who shares a family with you. Everything else you create is visible only to the members of the specific family, calendar, channel or conversation it belongs to. We do not sell your data, and we do not use it for advertising.']},{heading:'Who else sees it',paragraphs:['We use a small number of service providers to run the app: Supabase hosts our database, authentication, file storage and background jobs. Google, Microsoft, Apple and Facebook are used only if you choose to sign in with them, and Google also receives the calendar changes you make in Cluster if you connect Google Calendar. Expo delivers push notifications on our behalf, relaying them on to Apple\u2019s or Google\u2019s own infrastructure; a notification\u2019s content (a reminder\u2019s title, or an event\u2019s title, place and when to leave) passes through it.','Anthropic provides the AI reader of quick entry, which is part of Cluster Plus and runs only when you have it switched on. It receives only what \u201cTyping it in plain words\u201d lists, sent from our servers, never with your account, your email address or a family or member id. Anthropic doesn\u2019t use what it receives to train its models, and keeps it only for a limited period under its commercial terms, then deletes it. [Retention period: to be confirmed against Anthropic\u2019s commercial terms before publishing.]','Google Maps Platform provides address search and travel time, which are part of Cluster Plus, and are not available in the places listed under \u201cWhere travel time isn\u2019t available\u201d. When you search for an address, including when a place is typed into quick entry, the text you type is sent to it, with the country your device\u2019s region setting names so that places there come first, and when you choose a result, that place\u2019s ID; never your position. To work out a travel time, we send where the trip ends (the event\u2019s or reminder\u2019s place, as a place ID or as an address in text) and where it starts: the place of your previous event in the same way, or, for a trip from Home or from where you are, a position rounded to about 110 metres, never your Home\u2019s address. To find your Home\u2019s position, we send your Home\u2019s place ID, or the address you typed for it, once, and again at most every 30 days, for travel time or the weather (below), whichever is on. We also send how you\u2019re travelling, when, and which roads to avoid. None of this is sent for travel while you have travel time turned off. We send all of this from our servers, not from your device, and we don\u2019t send your name, email address or any account identifier with it. Google Maps Platform is not only our service provider: under Google\u2019s terms, Google may keep and use what it receives, such as search terms and coordinates, to provide and improve its own products and services, as described in the Google Privacy Policy (policies.google.com/privacy). Address search and travel time include Google Maps features and content, and your use of them is subject to the Google Maps End User Additional Terms of Service (maps.google.com/help/terms_maps) and the Google Privacy Policy.','On your way (Cluster Plus) also uses Google Maps Platform, and isn\u2019t available in the places listed under \u201cWhere travel time isn\u2019t available\u201d. To find where a stash item\u2019s place is, we send that place (as a place ID, or as an address in text). To see whether it is near a trip you are already taking that day, we send that trip\u2019s start and end as for a travel time (above) and ask for its route. None of this is sent while you have travel time or On your way turned off. As with travel time, we send this from our servers, without your name, email address or any account identifier.','Weather (Cluster Plus) also comes from Google Maps Platform, and isn\u2019t available in the places listed under \u201cWhere travel time isn\u2019t available\u201d. To show a forecast for an event\u2019s or reminder\u2019s place, we send that place (as a place ID, or as an address in text) to find where it is. For your Home, we send nothing more than the rounded position described above. We then send a point rounded to about 5 km to Google\u2019s Weather API. None of this is sent while you have the weather turned off. As with travel time, we send this from our servers, without your name, email address or any account identifier. Weather data is shown as \u201cIncludes weather data from Google\u201d, and is subject to the same Google terms and Google Privacy Policy. No other weather provider receives anything.','We do not otherwise share your data with third parties, and we do not use third-party analytics or advertising trackers today.']},{heading:'How long we keep your data',paragraphs:['Family content is kept for as long as your family\u2019s account exists, so the household\u2019s shared history stays intact. You or another member with permission can delete individual items, events or messages you no longer want kept.','Your Home and travel settings are kept until you change or remove them, or delete your account. Your Home\u2019s rounded position is kept for at most 30 days, and cleared as soon as you change or remove your Home. Travel times are deleted a week after the trip, and your upcoming ones as soon as you turn travel time off. Your device\u2019s position is never kept. Forecasts are never stored: they are held in memory for at most an hour and then dropped. The sentence you type for quick entry is not kept by Cluster; Anthropic keeps what it receives only for the limited period described under \u201cWho else sees it\u201d. A record of a Cluster Plus plan is kept after the plan ends.']},{heading:'Deleting your account',paragraphs:['You can delete your account from Settings \u2192 Account. This is permanent and cannot be undone \u2014 and it is not "everything about you disappears".','What is erased: your login credentials, your profile photo and display name, your device push tokens, your notification preferences, your membership in every family, chat channel and event you were part of, and any calendar you marked private, with the events on it. Also your Home (and its rounded position), travel settings, travel choices and travel times; and any Google accounts you connected, with the Google calendars you brought in from them (we ask Google to withdraw Cluster\u2019s access to each account first; nothing is deleted in Google).','Cluster Plus: your Individual Plus ends when you delete your account. A Family Plus you cover stays with that group until it ends, shown as covered by "Deleted member". The records of both are kept.','What is kept, because it belongs to your family, not just to you: lists, list items, calendar events, calendars, chat channels, chat messages (including group and direct messages), photos you sent in chat, photos you added as picture cards, and reminders you created or were part of. This content is kept and re-attributed to a generic "Deleted member" placeholder in place of your name, so your family keeps working after you leave it.','If you are the only owner of a family that still has other members, you\u2019ll be asked to transfer ownership before you can delete your account. If you are the only member of a family, deleting your account deletes that family too.']},{heading:'Children\u2019s accounts',paragraphs:['Cluster is not for children under 13. You must be 13 or older to create an account, and we ask for your date of birth when you sign up so we can check. We do not knowingly collect personal information from anyone under 13.','We do not keep the date of birth you enter. It is used once, at signup, to work out whether you meet the age requirement, and then discarded \u2014 all we record is that the check happened.','If someone under 13 does end up with an account, we will delete it. If you believe a child under 13 has created one, contact us at [contact email \u2014 to be added] and we will remove it.','A family can still give a member the "teen" or "child" role. Those roles control what someone is allowed to do inside a family; they do not change the age requirement. Anyone holding their own account, whatever their role, is 13 or older.']},{heading:'Security',paragraphs:['Data in transit between the app and our servers is encrypted (HTTPS/TLS). Access to your data is enforced at the database level for every request. No system is perfectly secure, and we cannot guarantee absolute security.']},{heading:'Your rights',paragraphs:['Depending on where you live, you may have rights to access, correct, export or delete your personal data. You can delete your own account and its personal data directly from the app at any time. For any other request, contact us at [contact email \u2014 to be added].']},{heading:'Changes to this policy',paragraphs:['If we make a material change to this policy, we will update its publication date and, where required, notify you in the app.']},{heading:'Contact',paragraphs:['[Company name, mailing address and contact email \u2014 to be added.]']}];function u(e){return(0,l.jsxs)(n.Card,{style:{marginBottom:n.spacing.lg},children:[(0,l.jsx)(n.Text,{variant:"subtitle",style:{marginBottom:n.spacing.sm},children:e.heading}),e.paragraphs.map(c)]},e.heading)}function c(e,t){return(0,l.jsx)(n.Text,{variant:"body",color:"textMuted",style:{marginTop:0===t?0:n.spacing.sm},children:e},t)}},2889,[11,13,78,1168,6]);
